## Git & Commit Standards

Use **Conventional Commits**: `type: description` or `type(scope): description`
(lowercase, imperative).

**Types:** `feat` (feature), `fix` (bug), `docs`, `refactor`, `chore`

**Scopes (optional; match the top-level area when used):**

- Top-level dirs: `addons`, `assets`, `cursor`, `hack`, `libs`, `plans`, `tests`
- `src/` buckets: `core`, `actors`, `world`, `ui`

Use a scope when changes stay in one area. Omit it for cross-cutting work
(e.g. `chore: update cursor rules`, repo-wide tooling).

Networking spans `libs/net` + `src/core` → scope as `libs` or `core`, not a `net` scope.

Enforced by `.githooks/commit-msg`. One-time setup: `./hack/setup.sh`
